.TH PGN-REVERSE 1 "October 2026" "pgn-reverse" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pgn-reverse \- reverse the order of chess games in a PGN file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pgn-reverse
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIINPUT OUTPUT\fR
.br
.B pgn-reverse
.B --help
.br
.B pgn-reverse
.B -?
.br
.B pgn-reverse
.B --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Portable Game Notation (PGN) file can hold one or many chess games. PGN is
widely used to save and share chess games played with friends, at chess clubs,
and in tournaments.
.B pgn-reverse
writes the games in the opposite order. The moves inside each chess game stay
in their original order.
.PP
For example, a tournament file may list the oldest games first, while you want
to study the newest games first. This command reads the existing file
.I INPUT
and writes a new file named
.IR OUTPUT .
.PP
.RS
.nf
pgn-reverse old-games.pgn new-games.pgn
.fi
.RE
.PP
The original file is left unchanged. INPUT and OUTPUT must be different files.
If OUTPUT already exists, it is replaced only when you use
.BR --force .
.PP
For four games, reversing changes their order like this:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Original order:
    1. Anna - Ben
    2. Clara - David
    3. Anna - Clara
    4. Emma - David

Reversed order:
    1. Emma - David
    2. Anna - Clara
    3. Clara - David
    4. Anna - Ben
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only the order of the whole chess games changes. The moves within every game
remain as they were.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pgn-reverse club.pgn club-newest-first.pgn
Reverse every chess game in the club file.
.TP
.B pgn-reverse --head 10 games.pgn first-10-reversed.pgn
Take the first 10 games and write them in reverse order.
.TP
.B pgn-reverse --tail 10 games.pgn last-10-reversed.pgn
Take the last 10 games and write them in reverse order.
.TP
.B pgn-reverse --head 10 --skip 3 games.pgn selected.pgn
From the first 10 games, skip the first 3 and reverse the remaining 7.
.TP
.B pgn-reverse --tail 10 --skip 3 games.pgn selected.pgn
From the last 10 games, skip the last 3 and reverse the remaining 7.
.TP
.B pgn-reverse --force --verbose games.pgn reversed.pgn
Replace an existing output file and show brief progress messages.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --head \fIN\fR
Take the first \fIN\fR chess games from INPUT and write them in reverse order.
If \fIN\fR is larger than the number of games, all games are taken. Zero
takes no games.
.TP
.B --tail \fIN\fR
Take the last \fIN\fR chess games from INPUT and write them in reverse order.
If \fIN\fR is larger than the number of games, all games are taken. Zero
takes no games.
.TP
.B --skip \fIM\fR
Skip games within the selection made by \fB--head\fR or \fB--tail\fR, then
reverse what remains. With \fB--head\fR, it skips the first \fIM\fR games in
that selection. With \fB--tail\fR, it skips the last \fIM\fR games. For
example, \fB--head 8 --skip 2\fR skips games 1 and 2, then writes games 8
through 3. \fB--skip\fR requires exactly one of \fB--head\fR and \fB--tail\fR.
.TP
.B --force
Allow an existing regular OUTPUT file to be replaced after the new file is
ready. INPUT is never replaced.
.TP
.B --verbose
Show brief progress messages while reading and writing. A successful run is
otherwise silent.
.TP
.B --help
Show command usage, options, and examples. No PGN file is opened.
.TP
.B --version
Show the version number.
.TP
.B -?
Show the same help as \fB--help\fR.
.SH "HOW SELECTION WORKS"
Without \fB--head\fR or \fB--tail\fR, every game is selected. The selected
games are always written in reverse order. \fB--head\fR and \fB--tail\fR
cannot be used together, and \fB--skip\fR cannot be used on its own.
.PP
Suppose INPUT contains games 1 through 8. With \fB--head 6 --skip 2\fR,
\fBpgn-reverse\fR first takes games 1 through 6, skips games 1 and 2, then
writes games 6, 5, 4, 3. With \fB--tail 6 --skip 2\fR, it first takes games
3 through 8, skips games 7 and 8, then writes games 6, 5, 4, 3.
.PP
If \fIN\fR is larger than the number of games in the file, \fB--head\fR or
\fB--tail\fR selects all available games. \fIN\fR = 0 selects no games, and
\fIM\fR = 0 skips no games. If \fIM\fR is as large as or larger than the
selected count, no games remain. An empty selection is a successful result and
does not create or change OUTPUT, even with \fB--force\fR.
.SH "FILES AND SAFETY"
INPUT is the existing PGN file. OUTPUT is a separate file that receives the
selected games. The original file is not changed, and the program refuses to
use the same file for both names.
.PP
An existing OUTPUT is left alone unless \fB--force\fR is given. Even then,
INPUT is never overwritten. OUTPUT must be a regular file or a new file name;
symbolic links and special files are refused. If no games are selected, an
existing OUTPUT is left unchanged.
.SH "PGN FILES"
Player names, tags, moves, comments, and variations are kept. The program does
not change the moves inside a game or check whether they are legal chess moves.
It only normalizes the blank-line separators: each written game, including the
last one, ends with one empty line.
.PP
A game without a result marker can still be used when its end is clear, such as
at the end of the file or before a new tag section separated by a blank line.
The program does not add a missing result or missing moves. It reports an error
with the game number and input line when the PGN structure is damaged or a game
boundary is unclear.
.PP
A recognizable tag typo is tolerated: the tag's closing quote has one
backslash immediately before it, followed by the final \fB]\fR and optional
whitespace to the end of the line. The tag is used to find game boundaries,
but its original text is kept unchanged. Other unclear tag errors are still
reported. With \fB--verbose\fR, at most ten repair warnings are printed,
followed by a count of any additional repairs.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success, including when no games were selected.
.TP
.B 2
An error in the command, input file, PGN structure, or output file.
.SH "TECHNICAL NOTES"
This is not a full PGN formatter. It keeps game text instead of rebuilding it,
but normalizes separators as described above. Comments between a game and the
next game are kept with the preceding game. A UTF-8 byte-order mark, if present,
is kept at the beginning. A partial selection omits other file-level text.
.PP
The program reads the file in order and stores game positions so it can write
the selected games backwards without loading the full file into memory. It
checks that INPUT has not changed during processing. Another program can still
change a file at the instant between the final check and writing OUTPUT.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pgn-count (1)
.BR pgn-list (1)
.SH AUTHOR
The pgn-reverse project.
